                <pb facs="0636" n="512" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg512"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Ic/10: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Ruddock</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-07-30T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 30, 1921—5
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>216. Your 268, July 21, 6 p.m.</p>
                <p>The Department perceives no reason for departing from policy outlined in its 184
                    of June 29, 5 p.m., 193 of July 12, 5 p.m., and 198 of July 15, 6 p.m., whether
                    permanently or for a term of years. You will ascertain from British Legation
                    whether it will support proposal outlined in Department’s 198 of July 15, 6
                    p.m., if proposed <pb facs="0637" n="513" xml:id="pg513"/> to the Diplomatic
                    Body. Reports just received from Hankow regarding looting of Ichang and Wuchang
                    state that looting was done by soldiers in transit or about to be demobilized
                    with no indication of anti-foreign feeling. Please advise of correctness of
                    these reports.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
